WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:18.000
1. Run your own ISSM script
Goal: upload a small MATLAB workflow and a synthetic JSON input, run native ISSM on your connected AWS resources, then inspect and download its results.

Have an approved CryoStack login, a verified BYO AWS connection, and configured MATLAB plus Connector.
Open CryoLauncher. Choose Cloud → Advanced → ISSM.

2
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:36.000
2. Download the tutorial inputs
Open Pilot Tutorials → Bring your own script and download the supplied native input packet. Extract it.

Scripts: runme.m, Launch.par, validate_launch_dataset.m.
Data: issm-shelf-small-v1.json.
Scripts are in examples/soft_launch/issm/. JSON is in examples/soft_launch/datasets/.

3
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:49.000
3. Create your workspace example
In Editor, enter a new name and choose New example.

This recording uses pilot-issm-script-1119. Keep the new owned example selected for the following uploads.
New ISSM examples include a starter runme.m. The supplied entry script replaces it.

4
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.008
4. Choose Upload
One JSON file is selected. Choose Upload.
The next view keeps the actual action and confirmation.

5
00:00:52.008 --> 00:01:04.008
4. Confirm the JSON upload
The selected-file count changes from 1 to 0 when Upload finishes.

The confirmation names issm-shelf-small-v1.json. Keep this dataset for the next reference step.
Datasets are stored separately from source files.

6
00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:07.008
5. Choose Reference in example
Select the JSON dataset, then choose Reference in example.
The next view keeps the actual action and confirmation.

7
00:01:07.008 --> 00:01:20.008
5. Confirm the referenced filename
The confirmation gives data/issm-shelf-small-v1.json.

This is the exact relative path read by runme.m. The dataset is now referenced by this owned example.
The referenced bytes are copied into data/ when the next run is staged.

8
00:01:20.008 --> 00:01:48.008
6. Replace and import all three scripts
Choose runme.m, Launch.par and validate_launch_dataset.m together.

Check Replace existing script files, then choose Import script files.
Watch selected count 0 → 3 → 0. Replacement is essential for the starter runme.m.

9
00:01:48.008 --> 00:02:02.008
7. Confirm all three files are saved
Read the confirmation: runme.m, Launch.par and validate_launch_dataset.m are imported and Files are saved.

The entry script now shows 37 lines, saved.
All three scripts and the referenced JSON matched the expected hashes before launch and in the submitted snapshot.

10
00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:13.008
8. Confirm the entrypoint
In Experiment, set Action to Run and Run target to runme.m.

Keep the owned example and the three saved scripts from the previous steps.
The selected entry script is runme.m; the two helpers and referenced JSON accompany it.

11
00:02:13.008 --> 00:02:29.008
9. Review the run
Choose Review & Launch. Confirm model ISSM, your owned example, and the resources shown in the review.

Use the existing configured license and Connector. Scroll to the bottom and choose Launch cloud run.
This native run used 2 vCPU and 8 GiB. The task time limit was 60 minutes.

12
00:02:29.008 --> 00:02:39.008
10. Follow the new submission
The new run enters Submitting, then Queued while AWS starts the container.

Wait for Running and Completed before opening its results.
The launch confirmation precedes this actual new submission.

13
00:02:39.008 --> 00:02:54.008
11. Completion and elapsed times
The native run completed successfully.

Submission → Batch start: 219.980 s.
Batch task: 45.010 s.
Script-reported interval: 2.156046 s.
Startup, task time and script time measure different intervals.

14
00:02:54.008 --> 00:03:09.008
12. Render the native velocity
In Workspace → Results, choose StressbalanceSolution and Vel, then choose Render.

This is the recorded Render action and its native output.
The selected output contains one diagnostic solution and 13 nodal values.

15
00:03:09.008 --> 00:03:27.008
13. Inspect the saved native field
The unchanged native PNG shows speed in metres/year.

13 vertices and 16 triangles. Speed ranges from 0 to 18.89046 m/year.

Node-RMS speed is 6.2386984 m/year. Convergence is the scalar 25.
The 10 km square uses one diagnostic SSA Stressbalance solve, without physical time advance.

16
00:03:27.008 --> 00:03:44.008
14. Download this run’s results
Choose Download results, then use the fresh HTTPS download link.

The saved ZIP has 13 CRC-valid members: mesh, native fields and figure, model, metrics and runtime/RNG provenance.
Keep the archive together with the input manifest and recorded runtime versions.

17
00:03:44.008 --> 00:03:59.008
15. What this example establishes
This script starts with zero velocity and fixes its non-front boundary velocities. The shared JSON speed setting is unused.

Thickness is source-confirmed at 300–500 m; its arithmetic-node mean is 400 m. The saved model geometry is opaque locally.
One verified native diagnostic execution. Full source/data/runtime receipts are linked; this is not a repeat or accuracy result.

